# A right leaf under a wrong parent, in 21 bytes, with every token agreed.
#
# `defp f(x) do x end` defines f. Outliner reads it as f being CALLED with a
# do-block: it hangs the `do_block` inside `arguments`, and stretches the inner
# `call` over it.
#
#   outliner                      tree-sitter
#   call [0, 20)                  call [0, 20)
#     identifier  "defp"            identifier  "defp"
#     arguments [5, 20)  <-- 20     arguments [5, 9)   <-- 9
#       call [5, 20)   <-- 20         call [5, 9)      <-- 9
#         identifier "f"                identifier "f"
#         arguments "(x)"               arguments "(x)"
#         do_block [10, 20)         do_block [10, 20)  <-- sibling of arguments
#
# Elixir's `defp f(x) do ... end` is `defp(f(x), do: ...)`: the do-block is an
# argument of `defp`, not of `f`. Outliner's reading says the block belongs to
# the call being defined.
#
# EVERY LEAF IS IDENTICAL. Both parsers agree on all seven tokens and on all
# of their extents. The entire disagreement is attachment, one level up.
#
# `plumb` scores this file **0 misread bytes** - there is no leaf whose name or
# extent moves, so a byte-indexed comparison has nothing to index. `rack`
# scores 15 of 21: 14 racked, 1 askew. That gap, in a file this size, is why
# 9.24% was a floor.
#
# This is not php and it is not a lexer gap: elixir seats its externals and
# builds 44,530 bytes with 39 unjudged. It is the corpus's largest single
# source of racked bytes at 17,654 - more than every other grammar except php
# contributes crooked in total - and 99.6% of it survives subtracting extras.
roots 1
mends 0
holds do_block
spans do_block 10 20
spans arguments 5 9
